{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Pushed as Gerrit change 2671 (commit 866e6dd6d46c). Recording the decisions and the evidence\nbehind them so they live on the ticket rather than only in the commit.\n\n## The mechanism question, answered with evidence rather than by picking the easy one\n\nThe ticket offered a runtime storage quota \"when compatible with current overlay2/root\nfilesystem\", else a bounded reaper. The quota is NOT compatible on this host and the daemon\nsays so in one line: overlay2's `--storage-opt size=` (and the `overlay2.size` daemon default)\nis refused unless the filesystem backing /var/lib/docker is XFS mounted with `pquota` --\n`--storage-opt is supported only for overlay over xfs with 'pquota' mount option`. That\nfilesystem is ROOT here, XFS reads quota options at MOUNT time and refuses them on a remount,\nso enabling it needs `rootflags=pquota` on the kernel command line and a REBOOT. That is the\nsame constraint S4 (`2ba3-bf77`) hit for /var/tmp, re-derived for this mechanism, and it binds\nhere too. Two further limits even after that reboot: the quota is PER-CONTAINER rather than an\naggregate ceiling over the share, and it applies at container CREATION, so every live service\nwould have to be recreated to acquire one.\n\n`container_quota_enforceable` publishes which regime the box is in, unalarmed for S4's reason:\nits honest value is 0 until that reboot is scheduled, and an alarm that pages continuously is\nmuted within a day. `container_reaper_active` IS alarmed -- that pre-empts the finding S4's\nplan review raised, since a byte cap enforced by a timer with no liveness signal can stop\nexisting silently while every other reading stays nominal.\n\n## What it does NOT guarantee, with the number\n\nStated first because it is sharper than S4's fill rate: THE REAPER CANNOT RECLAIM A RUNNING\nCONTAINER'S WRITABLE LAYER AT ALL. Only exited/dead containers are removable, so for the live\ncompose set this story delivers measurement and an alarm, NOT a bound. For the debris it does\nreach, the bound between two runs is `cap + fill_rate x interval`: at the 2 GiB share and a\n300 s period a sustained net fill above ~7.2 MB/s exceeds the share before the reaper next\nruns, and this gp3 volume does 125 MB/s baseline -- roughly 17x. A single runaway writer\ndefeats it; its real job is bounding steady debris accumulation.\n\n## Why it is NOT `docker container prune`, and how the live set was proved spared\n\nThe ticket names `docker container prune --filter until=...`. Its filter set is `until` and\n`label` only, with NO name filter, and that is not sufficient here. `autodeploy.sh` starts the\nmcp blue-green backends with a BARE `docker run` that compose never sees (`mcp_run_new`), and\nreaps them itself in `mcp_retire_sweep` under a guard this reaper cannot replicate: it reads\nthe nginx `/mcp/` upstream include and REFUSES to reap an exited container that is still the\nlive backend, because `--restart always` would otherwise have restored it. Reaping one anyway\nis bug `9ea3-7d07`: a transient exit became a PERMANENT 502 on 2026-09-02.\n\nSo candidates are enumerated and removed one at a time, sparing three overlapping sets --\nanything `com.docker.compose.project`-labelled, anything carrying `mcp_run_new`'s\n`rebar.service` identity label, and anything matching `^(rebar-mcp|compose-mcp-1)`. A test\nasserts that last pattern is byte-identical to `mcp_managed`'s own regex in autodeploy.sh, so\nthe two cannot drift. Two further guarantees come from the DAEMON rather than this shell:\ncandidates come from `docker ps -a --filter status=exited --filter status=dead`, which never\nlists a running container, and removal is `docker rm` WITHOUT `-f`, which the daemon refuses\nfor a running container -- the property `mcp_retire_image` already relies on for images. No\n`-v`/`--volumes` is ever passed and `docker system prune` is never used.\n\n## One budget, not a second cap\n\nADR 0112 and docker-storage-cap.sh's own header both anticipated this story: writable layers\nlive INSIDE /var/lib/docker, so two caps over the same bytes are either double-counted or\nmutually violable. `DOCKER_CONTAINER_WRITABLE_BYTES` (2 GiB) is therefore carved OUT of the\nexisting 20 GiB budget -- BuildKit 5 + writable 2 + images 13 = 20 GiB, images having been 15\n-- and container-cap.sh READS it rather than re-spelling it. When it cannot read it, it reaps\nNOTHING and says so: a reaper running against a guessed ceiling would delete containers to\nsatisfy a number nobody chose (autodeploy's `prune_docker_caches` takes the same position on\nthe BuildKit cap).\n\nThe 2 GiB is labelled as an ADR 0112 decision-6 starting point rather than a measurement: no\nwritable-layer figure was captured at the 2026-09-02 outage, since `docker system df`'s\nContainers row was never recorded.\n\n## Verification\n\n14/14 seeded defects were caught by a NAMED test, including the three protected sets, the\ngrace window, `-f`, the low-water mark, the guessed-share fallback, accounting-vs-enforcement,\npublish-a-0-instead-of-silence, a heartbeat gated on the docker read, notBreaching, a dropped\nalarm, the budget arithmetic, and an alarm on the quota reading. Two seeded defects initially\nescaped and both exposed genuinely weak tests, which were tightened rather than accepted: the\nlow-water-mark test now uses sizes at which \"reap to the ceiling\" and \"reap to 80%\" disagree,\nand the under-share test now uses 90% of the share -- the region between the stop target and\nthe trigger, where a token-sized container proved nothing.\n\nTerraform was written and VALIDATED (`fmt -check` clean, `validate` Success) and never applied.\nlint, typecheck, and the complexity and mechanism ratchets are clean (new=0 increased=0). 675\ntests pass across the new suites plus every existing suite that drives observability.sh,\ndocker-storage-cap.sh or compose-up.sh.\n\n## Known limitation, consistent with the siblings\n\nA change to container-cap.sh alone does not re-run its installer: `compose-up.sh` is in\nautodeploy's MATERIALIZER_PATHS but the cap scripts are not, exactly as for docker-storage-cap,\njournald-cap and vartmp-cap. The reaper unit runs from the installed copy under\n/usr/local/bin, so an edit reaches the box on the next compose-up. A stale unit is DETECTED\nrather than silent -- that is what `container_reaper_active` and\n`rebar-container-reaper-not-active` are for. Left consistent with the siblings rather than\nchanged unilaterally here."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788598129037242001,"uuid":"0fc0aaa3-def4-4a93-8a69-a76119c8794b"}